                  "content": "Table 3.1"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 16,
              "bbox": [
                0.102,
                0.653,
                0.92,
                0.763
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_2_table_16.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td>Element</td><td>Symbol</td><td>Type ofsurface</td><td>Hardness</td><td>Malleability</td><td>Ductility</td><td>ConductsElectricity</td><td>Sonority</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.777,
            0.724,
            0.843
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "On the bases of the observations recorded in Table 3.1, discuss the general physical properties of metals and non-metals in the class. You must have concluded that we cannot group elements according to their physical properties alone, as there are many exceptions. For example –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.848,
            0.723,
            0.882
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) All metals except mercury exist as solids at room temperature. In Activity 3.5, you have observed that metals have high melting",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
